          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A Hopf algebroid is an associative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebroid"} bialgebroid
            with an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
          %p
            A
            %em Hopf algebroid
            is a (possibly
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            (equipped with
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} space-algebra duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            with their relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            from groups to groupoids.
          %p
            Specifically
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+Hopf+algebroids"} commutative Hopf algebroids
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            as generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+Steenrod+algebras"} dual Steenrod algebras
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
          %p
            More generally there are
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroids+over+a+commutative+base"} Hopf algebroids over a commutative base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebras"} convolution algebras
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
          %h2#definition_under_construction Definition (under construction)
          %p
            In the general case we should distinguish left and right bialgebroids, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebroid"} bialgebroid
          %p In one of the versions, a general Hopf algebroid is defined as a pair of a left algebroid and right algebroid together with a linear map from left to right bialgebroid taking the role of an antipode.
          %h3#commutative_hopf_algebroids Commutative Hopf algebroids
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Aff
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi k
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff_k = Alg_k^{op}
            of affine algebraic
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+sections"} global sections
            over the scheme of objects and the scheme of morphisms have an additional structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+Hopf+algebroid"} commutative Hopf algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+equivalence"} antiequivalence of categories
          %p
            These
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+Hopf+algebroids"} commutative Hopf algebroids
            play a key role in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brave+new+algebra"} brave new algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+Steenrod+algebras"} dual Steenrod algebras
            for certain classes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and as such govern the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
          %h3#noncommutative_hopf_algebroids Noncommutative Hopf algebroids
          %p
            There are several generalizations to the noncommutative case. A difficult part is to work over the noncommutative base (i.e., the object of objects is noncommutative). The definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebroid"} bialgebroid
            is not that difficult and there is even a very old definition of an equivalent structure due Takeuchi. To add an antipode is nontrivial. A definition of Lu from mid 1990s is rather nonselfdual, unlike the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            and introduces rather ad hoc certain section map. So a better solution may be even to abandon the idea of an antipode and have some replacement for it. There are two approaches, one due to Day and Street, and another due
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabi+B%C3%B6hm"} Gabi Böhm
          %h4#noncommutative_hopf_algebroid_with_invertible_antipode Noncommutative Hopf algebroid with invertible antipode
          %p
            A definition of an antipode avoiding a section map of Lu, but requiring that the antipode is invertible. In this definition, given a left
            = succeed "-bialgebroid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H,\alpha,\beta,\Delta,\epsilon)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S:H\to H
            is an antihomomorphism of algebras with inverse map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{-1}:H\to H
            satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi β
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S\circ\beta = \alpha
          %p
            and for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\in H
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi h
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi H
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (S^{-1} h_{(2)})_{(1)}\otimes_A(S^{-1} h_{(2)})_{(2)}h_{(1)} = S^{-1} h\otimes_A 1_H,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %mi S
                  %mi h
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (S h_{(1)})_{(1)} h_{(2)}\otimes_A(S h_{(1)})_{(2)} = 1_H\otimes_A S h.
          %p Some authors call this concept a “full Hopf algebroid”.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#minimal_hopf_algebroid Minimal Hopf algebroid
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be a unital associative algebra. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes A^{op}
            has a structure of a Hopf algebroid, a
            %strong minimal Hopf algebroid
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(a) = a\otimes 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta(b) = 1\otimes b
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(a\otimes b) = (a\otimes 1)\otimes_A(1\otimes b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon(a\otimes b) = a b
            and antipode
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau(a\otimes b) = b\otimes a
          %p
            Lu (1996) considers this example an analogue of a
            %span.newWikiWord
              Poisson groupoid
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Poisson+groupoid"} ?
            structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ×
                  %mover
                    %mi P
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\times\overline{P}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a set. Thus she calls this example a coarse Hopf algebroid.
          %h3#scalar_extension_hopf_algebroids Scalar extension Hopf algebroids
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and a braided-commutative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yetter-Drinfeld+module"} Yetter-Drinfeld module
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\sharp A
            is the total algebra of a Hopf algebroid over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
          %p
            This construction is modelled on an earlier variant, first written out by Lu, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a braided-commutative algebra in the category of modules over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(H)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Drinfeld+double"} Drinfeld double
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The commutative version is classical, and there is an extensive literature, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroids+over+a+commutative+base"} Hopf algebroids over a commutative base
          %p
            Over a noncommutative base ring, there is a nonsymmetric version due J-H. Lu and a similar version is later studied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ping+Xu"} Ping Xu
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiang-Hua+Lu"}> Jiang-Hua Lu
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids and quantum groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 7
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arXiv.org/abs/q-alg/9505024"} q-alg/9505024
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=95e:16037"} MR95e:16037
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129167X96000050"} doi
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Drinfeld double and the Heisenberg double of a Hopf algebra
              = succeed ":3" do
                %strong 7
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1277953"} MR1277953
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1215/S0012-7094-94-07428-0"} doi
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ping+Xu"}> Ping Xu
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9905192"} q-alg/9905192
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200000334"} doi
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum groupoids and deformation quantization
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9708020"} q-alg/9708020
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum groupoids associated to universal dynamical R-matrice
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9811172"} q-alg/9811172
            %li
              blog discussion at
              %a{:href => "http://theoreticalatlas.wordpress.com/2009/03/18/a-note-on-quantum-groupoids"} Theoretical Atlas
          %p The modern concept over the noncommutative base has been discovered by several different people in several different formalisms. Some of the differences are merely cosmetic, but there are at least two main concepts, depending on the underlying concept of ‘bialgebroid’.
          %p Day and Street have a concept of Hopf algebroid here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B.+Day"}> B. Day
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Street"} R. Street
              = succeed "," do
                %em Monoidal bicategories and Hopf algebroids
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 129
          %p
            In this they start by taking an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebroid"} algebroid
            to be an “algebra with several objects” in the sense of a
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Vect_k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            of
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %strong bialgebroid
            to be a comonoid in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with linear maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(a,b) \to A(a,b) \otimes A(a,b)
          %p
            satisfying coassociativity, a version of the usual bialgebra axiom, and so on. On page 142 of the above reference they define an
            %strong antipode
            on a bialgebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to be a
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: A \to A^{op}
            together with a natural isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(b,c) \otimes A(a,S b) \cong A(b,c) \otimes A(a,c)
          %p
            A
            %strong Hopf algebroid
            is then roughly a bialgebroid with an antipode. With this definition, a Hopf algebra gives a one-object Hopf algebroid.
          %p A different and more widely used concept was developed independently in these two papers, which appeared on the arXiv within a couple of days of each other:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+B%C3%B6hm"}> G. Böhm
                ,
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em An alternative notion of Hopf algebroid
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 239
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0301169"} math.QA/0301169
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Street"}> R. Street
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B.+Day"} B. Day
                %strong 43
                (American Math. Soc. 2004) 187-226; arXiv:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0301209"} math/0301209
          %p and also described in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+B%C3%B6hm"}> G. Böhm
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.3806"} arxiv:math.RA/0805.3806
            %li
              G. Böhm,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K.+Szlach%C3%A1nyi"} K. Szlachányi
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids with bijective antipodes: axioms, integrals and duals
              %strong 32
              (11) (2004) 4433 - 4464
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0305136"} math.QA/0305136
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T.+Brzezi%C5%84ski"}> T. Brzeziński
              , G. Militaru,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Bialgebroids,
                  = succeed "-bialgebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times_A
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong 251
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0012164"} math.QA/0012164
            %li
              D. Chikhladze, Category of quantum categories, Theory and Applications of Categories
              %strong 25
              (2011) 1 - 37. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/25/1/25-01.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            This starts with a different concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebroid"} bialgebroid
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            gives a pseudomonoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^e = R^{op} \otimes R
            in the bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_k
            of k-algebras, bimodules, and bimodule homomorphisms, and a
            %strong bialgebroid
            is then an opmonoidal monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Hopf algebroid
          %p
            A class of examples of Hopf algebroids have as underyling algebra the smash product of a Hopf algebra with a Yetter-Drinfeld module algebra over it. These are the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+extension+Hopf+algebroid"} scalar extension Hopf algebroid
          %ul
            %li
              M. Stojić,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Scalar extension Hopf algebroids
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0219498824501147"} doi
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.11696"} arXiv:2208.11696
          %p An internal version of associative bialgebroids over a noncommutative base monoid in a symmetric monoidal category admitting coequalizers commuting with the monoidal product is studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+B%C3%B6hm"}> G. Böhm
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal bialgebroids, entwining structures and corings
              %strong 376
              (2005) 207-226
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0311244"} arXiv:math/0311244
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.48550"} doi
          %p The concept of internal bialgebroids over noncommutative base has been refined to an internal version of symmetric Hopf algebroids in
          %ul
            %li
              Martina Stojić,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal Hopf algebroid
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.14546"} arXiv:2308.14546
          %p This is actually an exposition of a part of the author’s earlier thesis where also a nontrivial class of examples of Hopf algebroids internal to a symmetric monoidal category of filtered cofiltered vector spaces is described:
          %ul
            %li
              M. Stojić, PhD thesis,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Completed Hopf algebroids
          %p A somewhat less canonical version of the same main subexample, written in coordinates, and with somewhat ad hoc treatment of completions (focusing on global cofiltrations only) is in
          %ul
            %li
              S. Meljanac, Z. Škoda, M. Stojić,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lie algebra type noncommutative phase spaces are Hopf algebroids
              %a{:href => "http://em.rdcu.be/wf/click?upn=KP7O1RED-2BlD0F9LDqGVeSHnmX9Ae5OTohRXxZ3Kuwuk-3D_fxWtIqIinsN9WJMaEHmGcrXGHheD-2FQtZ0bATf5S8uPqTE1vT5wX4Z3MHOr3LESbPy9zd7ZFfFy-2BgrWYwBzwnkToXTLfpXlfVLbWgoyxh3yG3X87hmFOy6WBB8-2BfFT6d5xCnkUAl78V3yBYjEJE6K0TYy2-2FYZewOjCVwZXxVneWJX8yW4YWbnghVE5d2d5zhz6lkzaj9LLNSVj-2B3QvKR6Dg-3D-3D"} enhanced pdf
              (free for online use)
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11005-016-0908-9"} doi
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8188"} arxiv/1409.8188
          %p A definition of a variant of Hopf algebroid which is somewhat similar to Lu’s definition but involves working with a 2-sided ideal, with help of a distinguished „balancing” subalgebra, is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zoran+%C5%A0koda"}> Zoran Škoda
              , Martina Stojić,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids with balancing subalgebra
              %strong 598
              (2022) 445-469
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1610.03837"} arxiv:1610.03837
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2022.01.027"} doi
          %p A notion of multiplier Hopf algebroid is studied in
          %ul
            %li
              T. Timmermann, A. Van Daele,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Multiplier Hopf algebroids. Basic theory and examples
              = succeed ":5" do
                %strong 46
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.0769"} arxiv/1307.0769
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927872.2017.1363220"} doi
              = succeed "," do
                %em Multiplier Hopf algebroids arising from weak multiplier Hopf algebras
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.3509"} arxiv/1406.3509
            %li
              Frank Taipe,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum transformation groupoids: An algebraic and analytical approach
              %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/tel-02288186"} link
          %p Further examples and counterexamples
          %ul
            %li
              Ana Rovi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids associated to Jacobi algebras
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong 11
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0219887814500923"} doi
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Kr%C3%A4hmer"}> Ulrich Krähmer
              , Ana Rovi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Lie-Rinehart algebra with no antipode
              = succeed ":10" do
                %strong 43
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927872.2014.896375"} doi
          %blockquote
            %p The aim of this note is to communicate a simple example of a Lie-Rinehart algebra whose enveloping algebra is not a Hopf algebroid, neither in the sense of Böhm and Szlachányi, nor in the sense of Lu.
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf-Galois+extension"} Hopf-Galois extensions
            of Hopf algebroids
          %ul
            %li
              Xiao Han, Peter Schauenburg.
              %em Hopf Galois extensions of Hopf algebroids
              (2024). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11058"}> arXiv:2406.11058
              ).
          %p
            On a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} *-structure
            on Hopf algebroids
          %ul
            %li
              Edwin Beggs, Xiao Han,
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shahn+Majid"} Shahn Majid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              %em -Hopf algebroids
              (2024). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21089"}> arXiv:2412.21089
              ).
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/algebra"} algebra
